## Repository Contents

This repository contains supporting technical material used during the implementation and testing of the honeypot environment. The files include configuration commands, service verification commands, resource monitoring commands, attacker testing instructions, and the guided command list used during the experiment.

The repository is organised into separate folders for the traditional Cowrie honeypot, the LLM-based honeypot, and the testing procedure.

---

## Folder Overview

### cowrie-traditional

This folder contains the commands used to install, configure, run, test, log, and monitor the traditional Cowrie SSH honeypot.

### llm-honeypot

This folder contains the commands and supporting files used to build and run the LLM-based SSH honeypot. This includes Ollama, the local LLM model setup, Python files, prompts, logging, and resource monitoring.

### testing-procedure

This folder contains the attacker instructions, baseline command set, and Excel command list used during the controlled testing sessions.

---

## Testing Procedure

The same guided command set was used to test both honeypots. The commands were selected to check system profiling, operating system identification, hardware and storage information, process and service enumeration, user/session activity, network inspection, filesystem discovery, and incorrect or misleading command handling.

This helped keep the comparison fair and allowed the responses from both honeypots to be reviewed using the same baseline inputs.

---

## Evidence and Screenshots

Screenshots were taken during the setup and testing process to support the recorded evidence. These screenshots were used to confirm the lab environment, IP addressing, service status, SSH access, log files, and resource monitoring outputs.

The main README contains only the overall lab setup and pfSense interface overview. More detailed screenshots and commands are placed inside the relevant folders.

---

## Isolation and Safety

All testing was carried out inside a controlled virtual lab environment. No public-facing systems, live organisational infrastructure, or unauthorised external targets were involved.

The NAT/outside network was used only during setup when Internet access was required for updates or package installation. During testing, the environment was kept isolated so that all attacker-style activity remained inside the controlled VMware lab.

